  "case": {
    "presentation": "A 68-year-old woman with stage IV BRAF-positive metastatic melanoma on dabrafenib/trametinib and anti-seizure therapy presents to clinic after cycle 3 nivolumab infusion 5 days ago, where she developed a grade 3 infusion reaction at the end of her infusion. She experienced nausea/vomiting, cyanotic hands, diffuse rash, bradycardia and tachycardia, fluctuating blood pressures, rigors, and diarrhea. All symptoms self-resolved. Her next nivolumab dose is scheduled in less than 2 weeks. Patient and family would like to proceed with treatment if safe, as there are no other treatment alternatives. Can the patient continue immunotherapy?",
    "recommendations": "This patient requires prompt allergy/immunology assessment in collaboration with oncology and pharmacy. The next nivolumab dose should be deferred until an expedited referral for Allergy evaluation, which can be done without preceding skin testing.  Re-challenge without a modified plan, such as desensitization, poses a risk of significant harm."
